UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|So the hand could have been defeated.  East should ruff low particularly since she sees the @S3 in dummy. Then when West gets in with the @HA, leads the last club. If I ruff with the @S3 East can overruff with @S6 and if I ruff high that might set up a trick for East.|
PG| ***** 54 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|On the other hand, as it turns out that doesn't beat the contract if I declare correctly. I ruff the club with the @SK and play a low spade to my Ace. Then unblock the @DK and play the @SJ. I end up taking 4 spades, a club ruff, 2 diamonds, and one heart for 8 tricks.|
PG| ***** 55 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However I may not find that line of play and playing @S2 instead of @S6 at trick three still is the correct way to defend.|
PG| ***** 56 ***** |


